{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Design Draft and Theory-to-Prototype Alignment","title":"Cognitive Load Analysis in the Draft","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting cognitive load analysis in the draft to cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-04-lo-01","statement":"Classify a screen's demand into intrinsic, extraneous, and germane load with evidence"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-04-lo-02","statement":"Attribute each load source to a specific feature, not a vague overall feeling"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-04-lo-03","statement":"Prescribe the type-appropriate move (segment, remove, protect) for each source"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-04-04-lo-04","statement":"Recognize when a \"reduce load\" suggestion would actually cut germane processing"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment","cognitive_load","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":4,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"04","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/04/03","inst-design/02/06"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_4_4_cognitive_load_analysis_in_the_draft_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_4_4_cognitive_load_analysis_in_the_draft_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_4_4_cognitive_load_analysis_in_the_draft_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_4_4_cognitive_load_analysis_in_the_draft_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-04-04-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_4_4_cognitive_load_analysis_in_the_draft_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_4_4_cognitive_load_analysis_in_the_draft_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_4_4_cognitive_load_analysis_in_the_draft_calibration"],"ail606-supplement-04-04-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_4_4_cognitive_load_analysis_in_the_draft_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-04-04-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_4_4_cognitive_load_analysis_in_the_draft_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-04-04-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_4_4_cognitive_load_analysis_in_the_draft_annotation_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Cognitive Load Analysis in the Draft\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Cognitive Load, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/04-04-cognitive-load-analysis-in-the-draft.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Cognitive Load Analysis in the Draft: Cognitive Load, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\n\"This screen feels overwhelming\" is a real observation, but it is useless as a design instruction, because it does not say what to cut. Cognitive load theory gives you the diagnostic vocabulary that turns \"overwhelming\" into a plan: some difficulty is intrinsic to the material, some is extraneous noise the design added, and some is the productive germane effort that actually builds understanding. The whole point of a load analysis is that the three demand the opposite of each other; you manage intrinsic load, eliminate extraneous load, and protect germane load. Cutting the wrong one (deleting practice to \"reduce load\") destroys learning.\n\nThis section teaches you to diagnose a draft screen by load type rather than by a single overall rating. You should leave able to attribute each piece of demand to a specific screen feature and prescribe the matching move: stage the intrinsic, strip the extraneous, keep the germane.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Classify a screen's demand into intrinsic, extraneous, and germane load with evidence.\n- Attribute each load source to a specific feature, not a vague overall feeling.\n- Prescribe the type-appropriate move (segment, remove, protect) for each source.\n- Recognize when a \"reduce load\" suggestion would actually cut germane processing.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA pilot tester stalls for two minutes on screen 3, the introduction to standard deviation. The draft margin note reads \"High, should reduce: delete the practice set.\" Deleting practice would indeed make the screen feel lighter, and it would also remove the only part of the screen where a schema gets built. The stall is real, but its cause is element interactivity (mean, deviation, squaring, and averaging all introduced at once), which is intrinsic load to be staged, not germane load to be cut. The right move lowers demand and keeps the learning; the proposed move lowers demand by deleting the learning.\n\nThe case is the central trap of load analysis: every kind of load makes a screen feel heavier, so a single \"feels hard\" rating cannot tell you what to do. Only a typed diagnosis distinguishes the difficulty worth keeping from the noise worth removing.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nCognitive load theory partitions the demand on working memory into three types. Intrinsic load is set by the complexity of the material itself, specifically its element interactivity: how many elements the learner must hold and relate simultaneously. You cannot delete intrinsic load without changing the objective, but you can stage it through segmenting, pre-training, and worked examples so it arrives in processable pieces. Extraneous load is demand created by the design rather than the content: redundant on-screen text that competes with narration, split-attention layouts that force the eye to integrate separated sources, decorative seductive details. Extraneous load has no instructional value, so the move is always to remove it. Germane load is the effort the learner spends building and automating schemas, exactly the productive work that practice and self-explanation create. The move for germane load is to protect it.\n\nThe diagnostic discipline is attribution: every load entry must point at a specific feature and a load type, because the prescription depends entirely on the type. The same surface symptom (a hard screen) routes to opposite actions.\n\n| Load type | Source on the screen | Right move | Wrong move |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Intrinsic | Four interacting concepts introduced at once | Segment; pre-train the mean first | Leave unstaged and hope |\n| Extraneous | On-screen paragraph duplicating the narration | Remove the redundant text | Reformat it to look nicer |\n| Germane | Two practice items after the worked steps | Keep; this builds the schema | Delete to \"reduce load\" |\n\nThe theory matters because total working-memory capacity is fixed and shared. Extraneous load steals capacity that germane processing needs; removing extraneous noise is not just tidying, it frees capacity for the learning to happen. That is why the order of operations is strip extraneous first, then stage intrinsic, then verify germane survived.\n\nModel the additive load equation against a fixed capacity. Total load is the sum of the three types, \\$L = I + E + G\\$, and headroom is whatever capacity remains after that sum, \\$H = C - (I + E + G)\\$. When headroom goes negative the learner stalls. Drag the sliders to see why cutting extraneous (E) buys back the headroom that germane processing (G) needs, while cutting germane just hides the symptom.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Working-memory load vs fixed capacity\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"C\",\"label\":\"Working-memory capacity\",\"min\":4,\"max\":10,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"chunks\",\"default\":7},{\"name\":\"I\",\"label\":\"Intrinsic load (element interactivity)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":8,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"chunks\",\"default\":4},{\"name\":\"E\",\"label\":\"Extraneous load (design noise)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":6,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"chunks\",\"default\":3},{\"name\":\"G\",\"label\":\"Germane load (schema building)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":5,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"chunks\",\"default\":2}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Total load\",\"unit\":\"chunks\",\"expr\":\"I + E + G\"},{\"label\":\"Headroom (negative = stall)\",\"unit\":\"chunks\",\"expr\":\"C - (I + E + G)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"E\",\"output\":1}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A screen overloads at headroom of negative 2. You can either remove a redundant on-screen paragraph (extraneous) or delete the practice items (germane), each worth 2 chunks. Both restore headroom to zero. Which move is correct, and why?\" explanation=\"Both moves restore headroom numerically, but only removing extraneous load preserves the learning. Cutting germane processing makes the screen feel lighter while deleting the very effort that builds the schema; that is lowering demand by deleting the learning.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Remove the redundant paragraph; cutting germane load would feel lighter but delete the schema-building work.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Delete the practice items, because fewer items always means less load.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Either one; the headroom math is identical so they are equivalent.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Neither; only raising capacity C can fix a stall.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWork through one annotated before/after revision of a cognitive load diagnosis table for a single draft screen.\n\n**Before (diagnosis table, screen 3):**\n\n| Screen | Load rating |\n| --- | --- |\n| 3 (intro to standard deviation) | High, should reduce |\n\n**Annotations a reviewer would leave:**\n\n1. \"High\" is one undifferentiated rating. It does not say whether the difficulty is intrinsic (the statistics itself) or extraneous (something the design added).\n2. \"Should reduce\" gives no target and no mechanism, so any later revision is untestable.\n3. The proposed fix in the margin, \"delete the practice set,\" would cut germane load, which is the effort that actually builds the schema.\n\n**After (revised diagnosis table, screen 3):**\n\n| Screen | Source of demand | Load type | Action |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 3 | High element interactivity: mean, deviation, squaring, and averaging introduced at once | Intrinsic | Segment into 3 worked steps with pre-training on the mean before the full formula |\n| 3 | Definition paragraph on screen while narration reads different wording | Extraneous | Remove the redundant on-screen paragraph; keep narration plus the labeled diagram |\n| 3 | Two practice items after the worked steps | Germane | Keep; this is the productive effort and should not be cut |\n\n**Why the after version is stronger:** it separates the three load types, attributes each to a specific screen feature, and prescribes the right move for each (stage intrinsic, remove extraneous, protect germane). A reviewer can now test whether segmenting and removing the redundant text lowered total demand without removing the learning. The change is a diagnosis, not a one-word rating.\n\nDiagnose screen 3 the way you would defend it, typing each demand source before prescribing a move. Predict before each reveal.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Typing the standard-deviation stall, source by source\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The tester stalls for two minutes on the intro that introduces mean, deviation, squaring, and averaging all at once. What load type is this, and what is the move?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Intrinsic; stage it by segmenting into worked steps with pre-training on the mean\",\"Germane; delete it to reduce load\",\"Extraneous; remove it entirely\",\"Intrinsic; leave it unstaged and hope\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"High element interactivity is intrinsic load. You cannot delete it without changing the objective, but you can stage it: pre-train the mean, then segment the full formula into processable pieces.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The screen also shows a definition paragraph while the narration reads different wording. What load type, and what move?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Extraneous; remove the redundant on-screen paragraph and keep narration plus the labeled diagram\",\"Intrinsic; segment the paragraph\",\"Germane; keep it because more text helps\",\"Extraneous; reformat it to look nicer\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Redundant text competing with narration is extraneous load created by the design. The move is to remove it, which frees capacity germane processing needs; reformatting it would not.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The two practice items after the worked steps are germane load, the productive effort that builds the schema. Keep them. Final order of operations: strip extraneous first, stage intrinsic next, verify germane survived. The margin note that says to delete the practice set would have cut the learning to make the screen feel lighter.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nNever act on an overall load rating; act on a typed, attributed source. For each entry in the diagnosis table:\n\n1. **Type check:** Is this demand intrinsic, extraneous, or germane, with a reason?\n2. **Attribution check:** Does it point at a specific feature, not the whole screen?\n3. **Move check:** Does the action match the type (stage intrinsic, remove extraneous, protect germane), and would it cut any learning?\n\nIf a proposed reduction would lower germane load, reject it; you are about to delete the learning to make the screen feel easier.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe classic error is conflating difficulty with bad design and trying to remove all of it; some difficulty (germane effort) is the entire point. The mirror error is rating a screen \"high\" without typing the load, which produces actions that lower demand by gutting practice. A third error is treating extraneous load as cosmetic; redundant on-screen text is not just ugly, it actively competes for the capacity germane processing needs.\n\nDifficulty is not the enemy; undifferentiated difficulty is, because it hides which demand to cut and which to keep.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk a screen feature by feature and ask of each one: is this demand coming from the content, from the design, or from the learner's own effort to understand? Content-side demand is intrinsic, design-side demand is extraneous, effort-side demand is germane. Naming the source forces the type, and the type forces the move.\n\nThen sequence the moves correctly. Strip the extraneous first, because freeing that capacity sometimes resolves a stall on its own and changes how much staging the intrinsic load needs. Stage the intrinsic next through segmenting and pre-training. Verify last that the germane work (practice, self-explanation) is still present and was not collateral damage.\n\nFinally, make each move testable. \"Segment into 3 worked steps\" and \"remove the redundant paragraph\" are checkable against a re-test of the same stall; \"reduce load\" is not. A load analysis is only as good as the re-measurement it sets up.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **claim** on the left, **evidence** on the right. The claim is the load type you assign a feature; the evidence is the specific reason (element interactivity, redundancy, schema-building) that justifies the type. By the end of AIL 606 04.04, your notes should let a reviewer check that no proposed cut would remove germane processing.\n\nA strong reader of a load diagnosis can answer four questions:\n\n1. For each demand source, what is the load type and why?\n2. Which feature creates the extraneous load, and what is the removal move?\n3. Where is the germane processing, and how is it protected?\n4. Which seemingly helpful cut would actually delete learning?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when every entry names a source, a type, and a type-matched move, and when no action lowers germane load. Treat a single overall rating as the weakest possible diagnosis. Where you are unsure whether demand is intrinsic or extraneous, mark it with the annotation tools and ask BigAL to contrast an intrinsic-load fix (segmenting) with an extraneous-load fix (removing redundancy) rather than for a generic definition of load.\n\nA useful final paragraph for the artifact would sound like this: \"I segmented the standard-deviation intro to stage its intrinsic load and removed the redundant on-screen paragraph to cut extraneous load. I rejected the suggestion to delete the practice items, because that demand is germane and is where the schema actually forms.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same diagnosis from novice learners to advanced learners who already hold the prerequisite schemas. Now the redundancy and worked-example supports that helped novices can backfire through the [expertise-reversal effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expertise_reversal_effect): for experts, the extra scaffolding becomes extraneous load. The element interactivity that was intrinsic for novices is largely automated for experts, so what was germane staging is now redundant. Transfer means re-typing the same features for a different prior-knowledge level, not reusing the novice table. If you understand load theory, raising expertise flips some supports from helpful to harmful.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/04-04-cognitive-load-analysis-in-the-draft-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"04.04\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence for AIL 606 04.04. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_cognitive_load_analysis_in_the_draft\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cognitive_load\", \"ail606_supplement_design_draft_and_theory_to_prototype_alignment\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm that your diagnosis table types every demand source, prescribes a type-matched move, and contains no action that would lower germane load. That separation is what makes the analysis a plan rather than a rating.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_4_cognitive_load_analysis_in_the_draft_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cognitive_load","stem":"A draft screen asks novices to read a dense paragraph while a narrator reads different text aloud. In a cognitive load diagnosis, this is best classified as which kind of load, and why?","options":["Intrinsic load, because the content is inherently hard.","Extraneous load, because the redundant on-screen text and mismatched narration force the learner to reconcile two sources, which the design could remove.","Germane load, because the learner is building a schema.","No load, because narration always helps."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Redundant or conflicting text and audio is a classic extraneous-load source introduced by the design. It is removable, unlike intrinsic load from the content itself.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_4_load_intrinsic_extraneous_confusion","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cognitive_load"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-04-lo-01"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_4_cognitive_load_analysis_in_the_draft_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cognitive_load","stem":"A teammate proposes \"reducing cognitive load\" by deleting the practice problems from a lesson. Why is this the wrong move in a load diagnosis?","options":["Practice problems never affect load.","Practice generates germane load, the productive effort of building schemas; cutting it lowers total effort but also removes the learning, so it is not the load you want to reduce.","Deleting practice always raises extraneous load.","Practice problems are intrinsic load and cannot be changed."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Not all load is bad. Germane load is the useful effort of learning. A diagnosis should cut extraneous load, not the germane load that drives schema construction.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_4_load_all_load_is_bad","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cognitive_load"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-04-lo-04"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_4_cognitive_load_analysis_in_the_draft_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","stem":"You ask an AI tool to label each screen of your draft as high, medium, or low cognitive load. It returns confident ratings with no source attribution. What is the strongest bounded use?","options":["Adopt the AI ratings as your diagnosis because they are consistent.","Treat the ratings as prompts to inspect, then assign each load to a type (intrinsic, extraneous, germane) with the specific screen evidence that justifies it.","Ask the AI to also redesign every high-load screen automatically.","Average the AI ratings into a single module-level score and report that."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A load diagnosis must name the type of load and point to the screen feature causing it. An undifferentiated high or low rating from AI is not a diagnosis the designer can act on.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_4_load_ai_rating_as_diagnosis","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-04-lo-01","ail606-supplement-04-04-lo-02","ail606-supplement-04-04-lo-03"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_4_cognitive_load_analysis_in_the_draft_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"Your draft introduces a complex statistics topic to true beginners and the intrinsic load is high. Which revision most defensibly manages that load without removing the content?","options":["Delete the hardest example so the topic feels easier.","Use segmenting and worked-example pre-training so the high element interactivity is encountered in smaller, sequenced chunks rather than all at once.","Speed up the narration so learners spend less time on it.","Add more decorative visuals to make the topic engaging."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Intrinsic load from high element interactivity is managed by sequencing and pre-training, not by deletion or decoration. The content stays; the demand is staged.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_4_4_load_intrinsic_extraneous_confusion","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-04-04-lo-01"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_4_load_intrinsic_extraneous_confusion","pattern":"intrinsic_extraneous_conflation","description":"Failing to separate load that comes from the content itself (intrinsic) from load the design adds (extraneous)","trigger":"learner labels a removable design flaw as inherent difficulty, or calls inherent difficulty a design flaw","feedback":"Ask whether the load comes from the material or from the presentation. Extraneous load is removable; intrinsic load is staged, not deleted.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_4_load_all_load_is_bad","pattern":"all_load_treated_as_harmful","description":"Assuming every form of mental effort should be minimized, including the germane load that produces learning","trigger":"learner proposes cutting practice or elaboration to reduce load","feedback":"Distinguish germane load, the productive effort of building schemas, from extraneous load. Reduce the wasteful kind, protect the productive kind.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_4_4_load_ai_rating_as_diagnosis","pattern":"ai_rating_mistaken_for_diagnosis","description":"Accepting an undifferentiated AI high or low load rating as a usable diagnosis","trigger":"learner adopts AI load labels without naming a load type or the screen evidence behind it","feedback":"A diagnosis names the load type and the screen feature causing it. Use AI ratings only as prompts to inspect, then evidence each one.","rail_action":"represent"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"c5188a70350b7be52a268bb7932bd7b9271a5cdaeabb8228509315cf0eae0b15","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}